Or would have been, had I not offered the amulet on the wrong altar. I 
put this down to not having played/gotten hold of the amulet itself 
for some years. ;)

The endgame was a bit of a farce, to be honest. I ran out of uncursing 
methods on the plane of fire, and a foolish keystroke left me sinking 
in lava. At this point, I panicked a little, as I wasn't *exactly* 
sure how you got out. Hit the ground with a wand of cold thinking I'd 
be able to dig myself out, and of course lava poured in because I 
hadn't bothered freezing the surrounding squares. Back to square. 
Worse than square one, in fact, being as at this point, my pickaxe was
welded to my hands, and I was about out of cold wands.

*Somehow*, I managed to dig my way out, and assaulted the plane of 
water armed only with a rusty pickaxe. The only reason I survived 
there was because of my ring of conflict, although it was terribly 
satisfying to see Rodney summon a bunch of monsters and then them set 
upon him.

Astral Plane, if anything, was even more slapstick. Managed to make my
way to *all three* altars, still only waving my pickaxe and zapping 
everyone out of my way with wands of teleport. Despite being pretty 
inoffensive, I was never actually in any danger of dying (three "oLS, 
none of which got used).

Why did I pick the wrong altar? I have no idea. :P Well, I was being 
extremely paranoid, thinking that if I stood on one to check its 
alignment, something might sacrifice me. Is that possible on the 
astral plane? ';' wouldn't reveal the alignment of the altar, sadly, 
but I did pick one which appeared to have a co-aligned high priest by 
it. Presumably, he must have been one of the victims of my earlier 
teleport blasts.

So, there you go. Nethack teaches me another important lesson about 
what *not* to do. No doubt the RNG is cackling now, having given me a 
ludicrously easy game (16 wishes total). :)

